An unordered, forward-facing backlog; completed work and its rationale
live in the git history of this file, docs/plans/, docs/design/, and
NEWS.

Items are listed alphabetically by plan name; the order carries no
meaning. Most items have an implementation plan in docs/plans/<name>.md
(a few record instead in a design doc or a differently-named plan,
named in the item), and the planning/implementation/review process (who
plans, who implements, who reviews, which gates run) is
docs/plans/README.md.
Gate labels (decision-gated, consumer-gated) rank priority rather than
block work (VD 2026-07-17); where a later VD stamp schedules an item
after 1.0-0, the item carries that stamp.
Standing rule (VD 2026-08-06): gate an item IF we cannot think of
anything valuable it might enable - that failure, not the absence of
a caller today, is the only licensed gating fact. "consumer-gated"
below reads accordingly, and the gate lifts the moment a valuable
enabled model or use comes to mind. An existing consumer is
sufficient evidence of value, never necessary.

Standing fact (VD): large datasets are common, and multiple chains
remain the default. The "single-chain n >= 1e5" spelling this entry
used to carry is an agent paraphrase VD disowns (docs/decisions.md
dec-B50, VD 2026-09-08). The large-n performance items are live, not
consumer-gated.

Standing discipline (from the 2026-08-07 census): verify an entry's
status claims against git and live code before scoping from it;
re-run a full census before release.

A "door" below means a recorded, undecided question, not a task.

items
  allocation-failure-residuals: two paths an allocation failure part
    way leaves unsettled, from review 4's slice S-A (the audit in
    docs/plans/review-2026-11-06/engine-robustness.md). A data
    replacement with another row count sizes the store, each chain's
    per-row storage, its response and its leaf covariates in turn, so a
    throw between them leaves them sized for different counts and the
    sampler unusable; staging every allocation before the store moves
    would settle it. The review's arm (80 rows to 60, every allocation
    failed in turn, then a sweep) ran cleanly with the right totals
    under ASan for constant leaves and a variance forest; linear and gp
    leaves were not probed. A standardization rederive, or a linear
    leaf's data replacement, leaves a saved draw the rewrite had not
    reached in the old basis; so does setOffset or setResponse with
    updateScale = TRUE, or rederiveScale, on a gp leaf, whose rewrite of
    the saved draws allocates a block-offset table per saved tree.
    Either way only predictions from kept draws mix units, after an
    allocation failure R reports as an error.
  alloc-test-container-overflow: tests/cpp under ASan reports a
    container-overflow in initializeChains in the alloc suite at the
    Makefile's link order; it also appears with every object built
    without the approximate-draws headers, and goes away when
    test_alloc.o links last. Undiagnosed: find whether it is a real
    overflow or an annotation mismatch between objects (found by
    approximate-draws S1, 2026-11-07).
  approximate-distributions: research arc, opened 2026-11-03 by the
    binomial family's large trial counts (dec-B567); plan
    docs/plans/approximate-draws.md on its branch. Ruled 2026-11-05: the
    default until the defaults study (dec-B590); the accept-or-keep route
    proven exact by independent agents before it is built, with a short
    vignette carrying the proof and a plain-R comparison of the samplers
    as the count grows (dec-B591); everything built ships in 1.0-0, the
    approximate settings opt-in, and the defaults study runs before 1.0-0,
    right after the build, and sets the default (dec-B592); the setting is
    a method name plus a tolerance (dec-B593). Covers the negative-binomial
    latent at large shape (docs/plans/nbinom-large-shape-pg.md, the
    September construction, background), real shape
    (negbin-real-dispersion) and non-integer logistic weights
    (weighted-binary).
  basis-string-name: forest(x, basis = "z") reads a single string,
    written or held in a variable, as the name of column z, at predict
    too, resolved as a basis name is beside a sampler's data (dec-B700).
    A character vector with one value per row stays a two-level basis;
    a lone string can never be a basis's values. Coupled with the
    basis-references plan, part 1. About 5 to 10 lines, tests and a
    help sentence; no fit that runs today moves.
  bcf-fallback-ess: the BCF equivalence harness's single-chain
    statistical fallback divides by the nominal draws of one
    autocorrelated chain, with no effective-sample-size correction, so
    it fails a seed-only change (17 to 20 of 20 runs on the latent
    scenarios) and cannot tell a seed change from a posterior change;
    found by latents-first's review (dec-A263). Batch means or an
    ESS-corrected z would fix it.
  bcf-latent-mixing: research door, no plan doc of its own; for the
    post-merge mixing research. The probit and logistic BCF chains mix
    slowly at large prognostic amplitude, and two gates carry that as a
    standing cost. The latent SBC arms stay out of the sbc.yaml matrix:
    their ladders fail the admission clause at |a| >= 5 on both links,
    with or without the amplitude rescaling move (docs/plans/
    bcf-latent-evidence.md, Decision 1). bcf-latent-exact.R's K = 3
    probit arm keeps the slow-chain statistic, its seed spread up to 7x
    its 400-batch se while unbiased pooled over 300 seeds, and fails
    about 3 quick runs in 100 on a correct sampler; left as is by VD
    2026-09-25 ("Leave it for now, but keep in mind that we have mixing
    work to do down the road and will perhaps want to revisit").
    Revisit both when a mixing change lands. The removed rescaling move
    is one candidate to re-measure there (docs/decisions.md dec-B127;
    restoring it requires the cache rule in docs/design/
    multiplier-combiner.md). sbc's bcf-probit arm shows the same cost
    in abs.a's ranks, a U (end-decile share about .27 against .205) on
    the sampler before and after forest-amplitude-normal alike (dec-B562):
    in the Cauchy tail, |a0| > 3, about a fifth of the prior, |a| has an
    effective sample of 5 to 23 in 150 draws, its autocorrelation time
    about 800 sweeps at |a| 5 and growing without bound past it; three
    times the burn and a start at the full truth (sbc.R's
    SBC_TRUTH_START) leave it (measured 2026-11-03). research-open.
  bcf-sigma-tail-mixing: research door, no plan doc of its own. In the
    EXTREME tail (|a0| >= ~40, ~3% of the prior) the chain is
    frozen-structure mixing that no amount of burn fixes; a principled
    remedy would target tree-structure mixing at high SNR (tempered
    early sweeps, restart-style grow proposals). Low priority - the
    regime only arises with a stale build scale swapped against a new
    response inside a larger Gibbs loop (setResponse
    updateScale = FALSE). Record:
    docs/plans/archive/bcf-sigma-residual.md sec 4 item 2. The gaussian
    SBC arm at its recorded settings (72000-sweep burn, thin 30, R = 200)
    passes sigma at 0.0602 against a 0.0917 band and flags prog3 and
    prog4 (0.1061, 0.1209) without the amplitude rescaling move
    (2026-09-24); it does not reach the tail regime this door names.
    research-open, decision-gated.
  binomial-test-trials: on a weighted binomial fit, a test row draws at
    its own k + f trials as its training twin would, a test-frame weight
    of 0 holds the row out (NA) and other weights do not change the
    trials; predict(weights = ) still gives new trials (dec-B690).
    Revises the call that drew test rows at one trial. About 5 lines,
    help and tests; test draws move only in the development build.
  capi-sampler-info: dec-B615. dbarts.h describes a sampler through one
    struct led by structSize, filled by one entry, holding every count
    and fact the one-fact entries report today, with the variance
    forest (dec-B601) and the Student-t residual as fields of their
    own; the one-fact entries are removed and stan4bart's calls move to
    it in the same landing. Each forest is reported through the
    caller's array of per-forest fact structs at a stated element
    size, a tree count at 1.0, mirroring the sketched forest
    description (dec-B639). Built in the host-neutral engine's header
    stage, before the freeze; its plan carries it at its next
    amendment.
  capi-unwind-protect: closed but for one arm. A host draw callback's R
    error is now caught at the callback itself, where R_UnwindProtect can
    still turn it into a C++ unwind, so the engine's run frames run their
    destructors before the jump resumes; the engine raises into R nowhere
    (the prior draws and the predict fan-out throw, and the bridge and the
    flat entries convert), and the chain rng buffer is released before its
    own raise. What is left is not the engine's: the rng library's
    unimplemented-kind refusals in external/randomBase.c and randomNorm.c
    are C and still raise through R from under a run. Both are default
    arms over kinds the library implements, so nothing reaches them
    today; closing them means an error channel out of the C rng API.
  correlated-outcomes: AR-1 / serially-correlated errors. Longitudinal,
    panel, ITS and DiD compose today via a latent AR-state + nugget in
    the outer sampler, driven through the setOffset conduit; EXACT
    pure-AR-1 errors
    are a deferred ENGINE door - banded residual precision plus
    cross-leaf GLS leaf updates break per-leaf independence. It opens
    on a consumer demanding exact AR-1 errors. Record:
    docs/design/correlated-outcomes.md (A). causal-motivated,
    decision-gated.
  dart-split-rule: dec-B605. dart() draws a split's column from all
    columns by default, as the BART package does, its split-weight
    update then exact; the exact augmentation (Linero's Algorithm 1,
    proven exact by independent agents) ships as an opt-in setting,
    built to the conditions the ruling lists, its name brought to VD
    with the build plan; a study compares the two at equal running time
    on interval coverage, RMSE and variable selection, with effective
    draws per second. Changes every dart() fit's draws. The empty-leaf
    veto stays and the help documents the prior the sampler runs
    (dec-B608); samplePriorPredictive draws that prior (slice S-C of
    the fourth review). No plan doc yet.
  dots-forwarded-weights: bart called through a wrapper that passes
    weights in ... followed by another named argument stops with
    "..3 used in an incorrect context"; found building frequency-weights
    S5 (2026-11-07), present before it. Forward the dots as the other
    front doors do, with a test through a wrapper.
  doc-freshness-guard-tightening: door, VD decision. The symbol rule
    in tools/check-doc-freshness.R accepts a cite when each component
    occurs anywhere in the target file, and the guard reads docs/design
    and docs/plans but not man/ or vignettes/ for content drift, so
    prose can go stale while every cite passes. Tightening either half
    (symbol inside the cited construct; content coverage of man and
    vignettes) will surface pre-existing drift needing a content re-pin
    pass before it can go green. No plan doc until ruled.
  equivalence-harness-seeds-axis: post-RC, re-record
    benchmarks/R/bcf-equivalence.R and multinomial-equivalence.R
    against independent per-scenario seeds in place of one chain's
    autocorrelated draws - a |z| bar over 40 correlated draws tolerates
    a shift of ~0.9 posterior sd per cell, which is why the statistical
    tier is decoupled from the gate. Record:
    docs/plans/bcf-cross-host.md sec 2.
  extract-predictors-not-samples: extract() returns only draws;
    extract(s, "predictors") is removed with the second basis landing,
    the package's six calls move to an internal reader, and the help
    points to model.frame(s$data) (dec-B692). About 10 lines, eight test
    files and two help sentences.
  fit-na-action-null: door, no plan doc of its own. At fit time,
    na.action = NULL falls back to na.omit on the formula path (R's
    model.frame) but applies no action on the matrix path, which then errors
    on a missing response. Found while planning predict's na.action.
  fit-na-action-weights-offset: door, no plan doc of its own. At fit time
    on the matrix interface, a missing weight or offset is refused under
    every na.action on gaussian and probit fits ("'weights' cannot be NA",
    "'offset' contains missing values"), where dec-A89 states that
    na.omit and na.exclude drop such a row, as the formula route does
    (its model frame carries both). 0.9-34 refused too. Found by the
    hurdle-completion critique (2026-11-03); that plan's hurdle arm
    follows dec-A89 on its own matrix route, the other families do not.
  gp-followups: sampled lengthscales and low-rank kernels; gp() takes a
    fixed positive numeric lengthscale today. Sampling one needs a
    theta prior, per-sample theta channels in the state and flatten
    formats, and costs multiples of the draw phase per sweep (kernel
    cache invalidation per theta draw). A decision memo comes first
    (docs/plans/gp-followups.md). Value holds: GP-leaf BART as a
    surrogate for sequential design of computer experiments (arXiv
    1203.1078), where a FIXED lengthscale asks the user to know the
    thing they are estimating, and low-rank kernels serve the standing
    n >= 1e5 fact. decision-gated on cost, not on value.
  gpu-bart: the backend-seam design waits on frontier items 4-5, the
    informed-kernel and preconditioned-CG prototypes
    (docs/design/parallel-bart-frontier.md sec 5). Memo and falsifiers:
    docs/design/gpu-bart.md.
  grow-from-root: frontier 3.1's exact informed-proposal use of the
    shared cut scan (docs/design/parallel-bart-frontier.md sec 3.1).
  hazard-setdata-surv: $setData on a hazard sampler takes a data object
    built from a Surv response and expands it onto the sampler's own
    periods as dbarts() does at creation; what a time past the last
    period does (refused or censored) follows the creation path's rule
    (dec-B694). Not sized beyond the
    door, its tests and help. Built with it: a hand-built person-period
    matrix whose last column is not named period is refused, $setData and
    new() checking it before the count, the message naming the two doors
    that fit (survival data at $setData, or a binary fit of the rows with
    the period a predictor), about 3 to 5 lines and the pins (dec-B703).
  host-wording-sweep: dbarts.h still says "host" for the code that runs
    the BART sampler inside a larger sampler, in about 7 header lines (the
    help, the vignettes and the R strings are done); a docs-only sweep.
  interaction-constraints: formal heredity (an ensemble lattice prior)
    - a fourth formal beside interactions(max.order, groups, forbid)
    plus a prior change. Value gate lifted: it enables
    functional-ANOVA BART (arXiv 2509.03317), for which dbarts already
    ships the hard-constraint half. Post-release, no fixed position: it
    happens, when is open (VD 2026-09-08, dec-B95). Owed there and not
    yet written: man/interactions.Rd states the two-stage workaround
    meanwhile, which today only the design doc does. Soft path-dependent
    penalties: gate STANDS, no model surfaced. Records:
    docs/design/interaction-constraints.md,
    docs/plans/archive/interaction-constraints.md.
  leaf-prior-predictive: no plan doc of its own; filed by the dec-A106
    ruling with docs/plans/leaf-prior-k-or-sd.md. A helper returning the
    prior spread of f at given x under a sampler's leaf prior, since a
    named sd is the sd of the leaf model's own parameter and is the
    prior sd of f(x) only for the constant leaf (linear: a lower bound,
    gp: an upper bound, monotone: a lower bound in the interior).
    samplePriorPredictive already draws f from the prior at x.test; the
    helper would summarize it, or compute it in closed form where one
    exists (constant, linear).
  matrix-fit-frame-test: a data frame given as test data to any fit is
    read by name as a frame fit reads it, the training names taken
    and extra columns ignored, a missing or renamed column refused as
    now; a matrix keeps its exact width. It applies at predict, at
    test = and at $setTestPredictor; the training-data setters keep
    dec-B478's refusal (dec-B711). About 5 to 10 lines, a few tests
    and a NEWS line; nothing that runs today moves.
  monotone-repair-merge: setPredictor, setData, setCutPoints and
    setState under forceUpdate, and the warm start, repair a monotone
    tree whose order a call breaks by merging leaves where it breaks, at
    their row-weighted mean; no call resets its leaves to 0 (dec-B693,
    revising dec-B278, dec-B355 and dec-B398). About 5 engine lines,
    zero-pinning tests rewritten and the monotone and setState help;
    draws move only for monotone fits after such a call.
  multinomial-response-shapes: bart() and dbarts() take the same
    multinomial response shapes (dec-B701): a data frame of counts is
    read as its numeric matrix, a non-numeric column refused, and a
    one-column label matrix as labels; a wider character matrix is
    refused at both with dbarts()'s message. About 6 to 10 lines and a
    few tests; nothing that runs today moves.
  old-fit-print-note: print of a fit saved by 0.9-x shows its call as
    0.9-34 did and then the refit sentence as a note, not an error;
    every door that needs the trees or the engine still stops with it
    (dec-B695, within dec-B109). About 5 lines and a test.
  pdbart-closed-arguments: at 1.1-0, when pdbart's BayesTree spellings
    are refused, replace pdbart's and pd2bart's ... with bart's usable
    arguments listed explicitly, pinned by a test against bart's formals
    less the excluded set (VD 2026-10-04, dec-B212).
  pdbart-hazard-compiled: a compiled routine for pdbart's survival-scale
    partial dependence on hazard fits, so a fine period grid (the
    default, from the observed event times) runs at sizes the fit
    itself does; the R route's work grows with the square of the
    subjects there and a size check stops it. Scheduled after 1.0-0
    (VD 2026-10-04, dec-B209).
  pdbart-ice: individual conditional expectation curves from pdbart and
    pd2bart - one posterior curve per row beside their average - so
    effect heterogeneity shows with its uncertainty (pdp::partial's
    ice, iml). Memory grows as rows x grid points x draws; a per-row
    posterior mean and interval keeps it bounded. Scheduled after 1.0-0
    (VD 2026-10-04, dec-B207).
  pdbart-per-category: partial dependence per category for multinomial
    and ordinal fits from pdbart and pd2bart - each category's
    probability as the variable moves, a category margin on the result
    and one line per category in the plots. Refused until then.
    Scheduled after 1.0-0 (VD 2026-10-04, dec-B209).
  prior-names-where-priors-go: inside forest() a prior's name (normal,
    fixed, student, invchi) means dbarts's prior only in an argument
    that takes a prior (amplitude =, and leaf.prior = at the fitting
    function); in sd = and the other number arguments a caller's own
    function of that name is called. dec-B272's refusal of a basis
    term calling a prior's name stands (dec-B708). About 15 to 25
    lines and a few tests.
  refusal-forest-label: every refusal names a forest by its label, as
    the fit's results do (its name in the forests list, else the text
    of its basis), at creation and at $setForest alike; a forest with
    no name and no basis stays "forest 2" (dec-B713). About 10 to 20
    lines and one test rewritten; no draws move.
  refuse-unsplittable-fit: bart(), xbart() and rbart_vi() refuse a fit
    in which every predictor holds one value on the rows kept and no
    forest has a basis, the message naming the columns; dbarts() and
    new() keep taking it, and a fit with a basis forest stays fitted
    (dec-B717). About 5 to 10 lines and a test; a 0.9-34 matrix call
    that fit silently now stops.
  sd-stated-meaning: getLeafPrior()'s sd.stated is TRUE wherever a
    forest's size comes from an sd the user wrote, the fitting
    function's normal(sd = ) or forest(sd = ), FALSE for the default and
    k, a sharer reading TRUE with its part (dec-B702). About 1 to 3
    lines, two help sentences and pins in a few test files; no draws
    move.
  setcontrol-forest-moves: setControl refuses, before changing
    anything, a tree-move mix its caller wrote that differs from the one
    in force when no forest would take it, naming $setForest(forest,
    proposal.probs = ); the same for n.perturb.cuts where a forest
    states its own; forest()'s help sentence that a stated mix is fixed
    for the sampler's life is corrected (dec-B697). About 10 to 15 lines
    and two tests; no draws move.
  setforest-roundtrip-sd: a setting given back to $setForest is
    compared with the value in force before the call, so a handed-back
    sd is no change on every model, whatever else the call changes;
    to pin an sd, state a different one or the same in a second call.
    This extends dec-B538 to a call that changes other settings too
    (dec-B714). About 5 lines, pins rewritten and a help sentence;
    draws move only for the handed-back call under a drawn k.
  setmodel-probit-stated-sd: a probit sampler of several forests whose
    candidate states an sd at creation refuses its own model ("does not
    resolve to the leaf hyperprior"), against the help's "always
    accepted"; a stated-sd install through $setModel cannot reach such a
    sampler. Found reviewing setmodel-installs-stated (dec-A278); on
    main before it. A fix and a pin.
  setstate-dart-zero: a state whose DART weight is 0 on a column the
    sampler may split on does not fit cleanly: unforced setState
    returns FALSE with the sampler untouched, forced installs it with
    the DART weights started fresh as installTrees does; negative,
    missing or all-zero weights stay errors (dec-B712). About 20 to 30
    lines reusing the warm start's step, tests and a help sentence; no
    state the package writes for its own sampler moves.
  split-probs-one-named: split.probs = c(x2 = .9), a named vector of one
    value, is refused as any named vector missing a column with no
    .default is, naming the missing columns and .default; today it
    reads as uniform with no message (dec-B716). With the rest of
    split.probs's doors, among them a hazard fit's refusal of a vector
    leaving out period, where n.cuts takes it (dec-B553). About 3 lines
    and a test; a 0.9-34 call that ran silently wrong now stops.
  stan4bart-constant-bart: stan4bart work. stan4bart refuses again a
    formula whose bart() columns each hold one value on the rows kept,
    with its no-bart-component message plus a clause naming the
    columns (dec-B715). About 3 to 5 lines in stan4bart and a test.
    Whether dbarts itself fits such a model is dec-B717.
  test-weight-missing: a fit whose test data carry a weights column with
    a missing value stops at fit time, naming the rows and saying to
    fill or drop them or to use predict(..., na.action = na.pass); a
    missing weight is refused at every door that reads one unless
    na.action asks otherwise (dec-B698). About 5 lines and a test; no
    draws move.
  variance-formula-one-grammar: variance = <one-sided formula> is read
    exactly as varianceForest(<formula>) reads it: terms matched to the
    fit's column labels, the dot and transformed terms taken, star,
    colon and I() terms refused with the message naming the fit's
    predictors (dec-B699, within dec-B517). About 5 lines, a few test
    rows and a help sentence; no draws move for a call naming columns.
  basis-references-to-data: a forest's basis is a reference to a column
    of the data, resolved by name where a data frame is given; the
    data object's per-forest bases become the package's record of that,
    dbartsData(bases = ) leaving the user surface (dec-B663, VD
    2026-11-07: "Use your new recommendation."). A design note first:
    what a pre-built data object keeps, dbartsSpec()'s resolution,
    predict's bases =, the matrix interface, the tests using bases =;
    it comes back as a decision. Interim: dbartsSpec() refuses a data
    object carrying bases beside a list declaring one, as dbarts() does.
    The object keeps the data frame whole and as little derived from it
    as it can, the quantized predictor block made from the frame when a
    sampler needs it (dec-B670, VD 2026-11-07: "keep the data frame, but
    we'll want to be careful what derivative data objects we keep from
    it"). dbartsData(keep.data = FALSE) keeps no frame, the default
    keeping it (dec-B674, VD 2026-11-08: "(a), but I would critically
    examine what bartCause and stan4bart do now versus what they should
    do"): what the two packages pass and read is settled by that
    examination, not by their current code. On an object that keeps
    its frame the response, weights and offsets are made from the frame
    too, with no copy stored, o@y and its kin naming accessors that read
    them from there (dec-B675, VD 2026-11-08: "No, I want (a). It should
    be read from there."). There o@x holds a stand-in that stops when
    read, naming the accessor (dec-B676, VD 2026-11-08: "Sure, (a).").
    A data object's data is read with model.frame(o), its rows counted
    with nobs(o); extract() gets no data-object method (dec-B677, VD
    2026-11-08: "OK, do that."). Where a sampler is made the caller's
    frame is dropped and the sampler's data holds the model frame and
    what its forests need, the setters writing into it (dec-B678, VD
    2026-11-08: "Yes, of course, the model frame. And whatever we need
    for forests."); the note designs and sizes it.
  basis-one-column-labels: a one-column character, logical or factor
    matrix given as a basis is read as its column, two levels, at every
    door; wider ones stay refused (dec-B662, VD 2026-11-07: "We can use
    (b)."). About 5 lines, 19 test lines rewritten, a help sentence in
    dbartsData and forest.
  starting-sigma-fallback-zero-weights: where no regression is defined
    a weighted fit's starting sigma becomes the weighted residual sd of
    the intercept alone over the rows of positive weight, sd(y) at unit
    weights, on both routes and in xbart's folds; the fewer-than-two-
    values fallback stays unweighted (dec-B660, VD 2026-11-07: "Yes, do
    (b)."). About 5 lines, one pin moved (1.528 to 1.871), one test of
    weights 100.
  state-basis-levels-by-name: owed when a factor basis may have three
    or more levels: a pin of the permutation's direction in a state
    install by level name (dec-B647, dec-A277), which two levels cannot
    test.
  study-firming: every study whose result decides a default, a sampler
    change or a gate is brought, in time, to what published research
    of its kind would do (dec-B637); k-move-pricing is the first. In
    order:
    - drop of the swap move from the default mixture (dec-B02): rerun
      at the defaults (75 trees, 4 chains, 20 or more seeds) with
      rank-normalized bulk and tail effective draws, effective draws per
      second, and coverage and length for f, before the release, since
      NEWS says it "costs nothing measurable";
    - the logistic k default: reread the October study's stored fits,
      whose truths were probit surfaces; redesign the September rerun
      (dec-B390, dec-B634) on Chipman, George and McCulloch (2010)'s
      lines, with a cross-validated comparison on real data;
    - the negative binomial default (nbinom-default-prior): reread the
      stored fits for RMSE, bias and coverage per real dataset, and
      score Murray's published rule;
    - stan4bart's initial fit (dec-B441) and the starting sigma on
      forest bases (dec-B603): widen each to several designs;
    - the monotone defaults: reread the stored runs against nominal;
    - each planned study (dart's split rule, the variance-forest
      calibration, the Polya-Gamma defaults) designed to the standard
      from the start;
    - the probit k default's cross-validated arm, then the internal
      gates: the SBC comparison rule and the equivalence harness's
      false-alarm rate;
    - the rest, minor: the Carnegie DOI in
      docs/design/grow-from-root-default.md is wrong (10.1214/18-STS682).
  warm-start-across-models: a warm start from a donor that is another
    model, such as a fast fit on a transformed response seeding a slower
    model on the original scale, with whatever scale or dispersion the
    two do not share translated. After 1.0-0 (VD 2026-10-05, dec-B248).
  interrupt-gaps: what a user's interrupt still does not reach or
    reaches as an error (docs/plans/interrupt-is-interrupt.md, landing
    note). Not polled at all: $sampleTreesFromPrior,
    $sampleLeafParametersFromPrior, predict and the R-side latent
    refresh. On the sweep-callback entry a real signal or a time limit
    arrives while the closure runs under R's evaluator and comes out as
    "error evaluating the sweep callback": evaluate the closure under a
    handler for interrupts and errors, as the poll does, an interrupt
    canceling the run and an error keeping its message. The entry has
    no caller in R/ today.
  warm-start-by-position: a warm start matches the donor's columns and
    a factor's levels to the recipient's by position, not by name: a
    donor fitted with a factor's levels reversed installs with no
    word and starts at a fit that correlates -0.9995 with the donor's
    (run 2026-10-11, docs/design/warm-start-salvage.md when it
    lands). Decide: match by name where both carry names, refuse a
    mismatch, or document. With the warm start's slices.
  warm-start-linear-covariates: a stored state does not record which
    covariates a linear leaf's slopes belong to, so a linear donor on
    one covariate installs into linear leaves on another with no word
    (run 2026-10-11). With the warm start's slices.
  warm-start-ordered-labels: a warm-start donor's split on an ordered
    factor is matched by label to the recipient's cut between the same
    two labels; a split with no single place is declined unforced and
    cut back forced, a numeric donor column there likewise (dec-B664,
    VD 2026-11-07: "Use your recommendation for this item."). Not sized.
  warm-start-bart-families: bart(family = "nbinom", "ordinal" or
    "multinom", warm.start = ) is refused at the bart level ("does not
    support 'warm.start'"), where the sampler's installTrees takes a
    donor of any of the 7 families from any other. Lift where the
    install is sound, or say why not. With the warm start's slices.
  warm-start-undo-test: Sampler::installForests undoes a refused warm
    start by snapshotting each touched chain's state and setting it back
    (restoreTouched), and nothing tests it. Every refusal it guards is
    checked before any chain is touched, so the undo runs only on an
    allocation failure or a rebuild that passed its pre-check and then
    failed; neither can be reached from tinytest or tests/cpp without a
    fault-injection hook. Add one (a tests/cpp peer that fails the Nth
    installForest) and check the recipient is restored: trees, sigma, k,
    DART state, transform and the next draws.
  saved-sampler-data-twice: a saved sampler on main writes its data
    twice, once in its data object and once through its engine pointer:
    4.55 MB against 2.32 MB in 0.9-34 at the basis-references design's
    sizing run (found 2026-11-08). A regression against 0.9-34, so not
    deferred past the release. Whether the bridge needs the data kept
    behind the pointer is an engine question; examine it before the
    basis-references build, which changes what the data object holds.
  setdata-repeated-names: setData and setPredictor read a sampler whose
    own column names repeat (x, b, x, d) by name only for the same names
    in the same places; the same names in another order are refused,
    since a repeated name names two places. A rename (x, b, y, d) was
    refused with "the data's column 'x' is in the place of the sampler's
    'x'" and is now refused as names that are not the sampler's
    (dec-B478). Found by the review of state-frame-prior slice 2. Not
    planned.
  setdata-own-data-ulp: setData(s$data) holds the stored state and the
    records bit for bit (state-frame-prior slice 3), but the draws that
    follow differ from an untouched twin's by about 1e-16, the fits
    being rebuilt for the data handed in; the build before does the
    same. Found by that slice's review. Not planned.
  code-matrix-category-count: a sampler made from a matrix of codes
    records no level table, so a re-creation infers each factor's count
    from the codes present: copy() is refused after setData of fewer
    codes, and such a sampler handed a data frame takes the frame's
    table into its record, which a copy then reads as its count. Record
    the count such a sampler was made with, and decide what a frame
    handed to it means (docs/plans/factor-levels-across-setdata.md).
    Since state-frame-prior slice 1 the ordered case refuses too: a
    column declared ordered through attr(x, "varTypes") and handed
    fewer codes by setData leaves the record the grid of the levels the
    sampler was made with, which copy() and a reload refuse, where
    before the copy was made and the reload ran; a carried level grid
    setting the level count at creation would close it for ordered
    columns.
    Ruled: the sampler records each factor column's level count at
    creation, from the codes where no level table comes with them, and
    every re-creation reads it, ordered and unordered alike (dec-B672,
    VD 2026-11-07: "Do (b)."). About 15 to 30 lines and 3 or 4 tests;
    no running sampler's draws move. What a frame handed to such a
    sampler means stays open here.
  k-move-pricing: after the k study lands, before 1.0-0 (dec-B530, VD
    2026-10-30: "Sure, let's try it."). Prices the collapsed move under
    logistic as a sequence, each step's rule fixed before any fit: (1)
    the move's small-k tail is right, on the simulated small-sample,
    near-separation and rare-outcome regimes with k drawn from the
    prior, and if it fails pricing stops; (2) it wins at equal time on
    coverage (VD 2026-10-17: calibration the target, over-coverage
    counting as error, on interesting real problems), on those regimes
    and on fits with no strong signal, which also gives the real
    benchmark of its cost: if so, the move is on for every logistic
    fit, with no trigger; (3) only if that cost is too high, a check on
    the data, its form fixed on the simulated regimes alone and tested
    on real logistic datasets not yet looked at, the 22 of the k study
    having been seen; (4) an opt-in if both fail. Not sized; a rough
    guess of tens of core-hours, mostly step 2. Logistic fits only;
    probit keeps its own rescaling step (dec-B617); the move reaches
    the forests sharing one prior total (dec-B618) and hazard.logistic
    and count-weighted logistic fits, timed, not scored (dec-B619);
    cost too high is harm at equal time only without strong signal, or
    a timed cell past 1.25 times shipped (dec-B620); step 2 is the move
    against shipped head to head at equal time, no burn-in-only arm
    (dec-B621), and how long each takes to converge is measured as
    published sampler comparisons measure it (dec-B622). Record
    docs/plans/binary-k-mixing-need.md, What the result leads to;
    move on wt/collapsed-move-plan, plan
    docs/plans/collapsed-scale-move.md.
    Must be written before pricing starts. Proposed by the independent
    critique of the k study, none of them a ruling:
    - Proposal: Step 1, a prior tilted toward small k: only 5 of 1,040
      true k fall under 0.05, so the step as stated cannot fail. Run
      calibration with a tilted prior in both the truth and the fit;
      chi(1.5, 0.1) puts about 22 percent of true k under 0.05. About a
      core-hour. Require rank uniformity and coverage of near-0-or-1
      rows there, plus numerics.
    - Proposal: State the length of any told fit read at a small true
      k. Near 0 or 1 near separation it reads, below a true k of 0.5,
      0.890 at ten times the default under logistic, but 0.803 at ten
      times and 0.869 at forty under probit, so a step reading it under
      probit, or under a prior tilted further toward small k, sizes its
      length first.
    - Proposal: A burn-in-only arm: moving k only during burn-in costs
      almost nothing per sweep and may make step 3 moot; the plan lists
      it and the sequence does not.
    - Proposal: Held-out scores as a gate, with the datasets named:
      coverage has no truth on real outcomes, and held-out outcomes do.
      An exploratory probe has been seen on 20 datasets, so those are
      spent for this purpose. About 1 to 2 core-hours.
    - Proposal: What "wins" means: absolute error from nominal, by
      estimand class, both pooled over rows and per fit.
    - Proposal: The no-harm margin on fits with no strong signal,
      checked against nominal and not against the told fit, which itself
      sits about 1 point low under logistic.
    - Proposal: How equal time is measured.
    - Proposal: A number for "cost too high".
    - Proposal: Whether the probit half stays out: dec-B530 speaks of
      logistic fits and is silent on it.
    - Note, from the maintainer's question at dec-B555: the study's
      "k's" means that getting k right closes the gap with today's tree
      moves, not that the trees are clear. Whether better tree moves
      close the same gaps was not run, and all seven problems are on
      probabilities and bands on them, none on an effect, so a gain on
      effects is not what this evidence predicts.
    - Proposal: Whether the move's code size, 1,400 to 2,900 lines with
      a slice that changes the posterior, is a gate or only reported.
