  nbinom-default-prior: the negative binomial default stays k drawn
    under chi(1.5, 2) for now, and the question is not closed (dec-B558,
    VD 2026-11-02: "I don't feel like we're done with this, but we can
    keep (a) for now."). The redesigned study (dec-B432) kept it under
    its rule; two referees returned major revision and neither supports
    a change on its evidence. At real spreads the priors barely differ,
    what decided lay in small stretched cells, and the prior still fails
    the prior check. Reopening takes a study to the standard of dec-B559:
    arguing as others argue for default priors (Murray's data-scaled rule
    for log-linear BART was never scored), on estimands natural to each
    dataset, with RMSE, bias and heterogeneous effects scored. Some
    failures are not a prior's: on the study's confounded design, error
    in individual effects is 0.218 against a true effect sd of 0.183, and
    group means cover 0.456, under either prior. The finding:
    docs/design/nbinom-default-study.md; the first study's note:
    docs/design/nbinom-prior-predictive.md.
  per-chain-responses: for VD to schedule (dec-B419, VD 2026-10-09;
    note docs/design/per-chain-responses.md). Responses and offsets
    held per chain so one sampler holds every chain of a larger Gibbs
    sampler (stan4bart today runs one sampler per chain). The note
    recommends one transform fixed at creation from the response less
    the intercept-free initial fit, with updateScale on per-chain data
    re-deriving one transform from every chain; on the one model shape
    it measured, warm-up re-derivation bought nothing. A stress study
    over five further designs (scratch/s4stress/, 2026-10-10, before
    the maintainer) found one, many small groups with group-level
    covariates in the forest, where a creation-fixed transform is 0.6
    of where chains settle and the split between the forest and the
    random effects is worse. Direction ruled: one mapping fixed at
    creation from a richer initial fit (the initial lme4 fit given
    BART's covariates as linear fixed effects) (dec-B437, VD
    2026-10-10); that mapping's design (docs/design/stan4bart-response-
    range.md: factor covariates, failed and slow fits, the fallback) was
    ruled the same day (dec-B441, dec-B444 to dec-B447) and is built in
    stan4bart. What remains is the per-chain data itself, whose stan4bart
    motivation is now one sampler for every chain (threads, not
    processes) and a smaller saved fit. About 1260 lines planned, after
    response-scale-rows.
  variance-forest-prior-calibration: a study (dec-B597, VD 2026-11-06;
    the dec-B559 standard for changing a default prior). A variance
    forest's per-tree priors match the written chisq(df, quantile)'s
    prior mean, which exists only above df 2, so two or more variance
    trees refuse df of 2 or below. Matching the spread of log sigma
    squared instead is defined for every df above 0 and nearly agrees
    at the default (40 trees: per-tree df 87 against 74 at df 3, within
    2 percent above df 5); at df 2.01 the mean rule's log spread is 2.30
    against the written prior's 1.28. Compare the two rules' fits and
    intervals; adopting log matching lifts the refusal and moves every
    variance-forest fit.
  variance-forest-tree-shift: after 1.0-0 (dec-B500, VD 2026-10-29).
    The tree-shift step acts on the mean forests only; varianceForest()
    takes no treeShift. Measure how a frozen variance forest's leaf
    values mix (structures held, leaves redrawn) and, only if slow,
    derive the multiplicative step - each tree's leaves scaled by
    factors whose product is one, which under inverse-gamma leaves has
    no closed-form draw - with its exactness gate; varianceForest()
    then takes treeShift.
