    amplitude stated on the term go, vars, and the list door.
  drawn-coefficient-start-by-kind: a drawn coefficient on a forest with
    no basis or a factor starts by its forest's position (1, and 0 for
    the second forest's first level); a start by kind, the cleaner rule,
    moves the draws of every model of three forests. A numeric one
    starts at 1 (forest-multiplier, push 2). Each is then multiplied once
    by its start scale, 1 / 0.674 with a basis and the unit over sd with
    none, which keeps the older units' chain (forest-amplitude-normal).
    A start far from where the data put the coefficient burns in slowly:
    on one gaussian data set a numeric forest stated at about 40 times
    the size the data support starts |a| at 1.48 and settles near 0.027
    only after 6,000 to 20,000 sweeps, a fresh sampler and one written
    live alike, where at a tenth of that sd it settles within 2,000
    (measured 2026-11-03; a live write leaves no prior state creation
    would not, and this burn-in was the gap a first probe read as one).
    Not planned.
  prior-predictive-several-forests: samplePriorPredictive refuses a
    sampler of several forests by name; drawing there needs the
    coefficients drawn from their prior, which dec-B361's rule would
    then cover. Not planned.
  several-column-basis-meaning: what a basis of several numeric
    columns means, shorthand for a forest each or one shared shape
    times a weighted sum (the curve-times-modifier model of a spline
    basis is the second), with how a size is stated for each column
    (dec-B272). After 1.0-0 (dec-B282).
  held-coefficients-in-state: a state from a sampler that draws a
    two-level factor's coefficients installs into one that holds them,
    in silence: the trees arrive, the coefficients stay 0 and 1, and
    the install reports itself exact (test-state-not-model.R pins the
    rule as "fixed amplitudes"; critique of forest-multiplier,
    section 10). After the multiplier slice's held push it is the one
    place left where a sampler runs trees drawn under other held
    values. Not planned.
  rederive-same-range-ulp: a re-derivation that finds the range in
    force again is not a no-op in bits. GaussianResponse::setOffset
    and setResponse at updateScale = TRUE take sigma and its prior
    through * range_ then / range_, which can move sigma by one ulp
    (run 2026-10-11 on d5b637ee): after setOffset(offset in force,
    updateScale = TRUE) and no other change, 15 of 200 one-chain
    gaussian samplers then draw differently from an untouched twin,
    setResponse on the same 15 seeds; with two chains and an offset,
    7 of 25 gaussian, 11 of 25 Student-t and 4 of 25 aft; at most
    2.5e-15 in a draw over 1000 sweeps. updateScale = FALSE is inert
    (200 of 200), and probit, logistic, ordinal and nbinom are inert
    at TRUE (0 of 25 each). setWeights and setActiveRows with
    updateScale = TRUE inherit it (docs/plans/response-scale-rows-c.md).
    setData at a held range (state-frame-prior slice 3) installs the
    response under the transform in force and leaves them alone.
    Leaving sigma and its prior alone where the range did not move
    shifts draws of both response setters, so it takes that class's
    re-record. After 1.0-0 unless a slice touching that function takes
    it.
  bartcause-data-route: from the examination dec-B674 asked for
