host-neutral engine (decided, VD 2026-09-08; docs/decisions.md dec-B85)
  after the merge to main, before the 1.0-0 CRAN submission (VD
  2026-09-23: an interface change consumers build against, so it lands
  before the header becomes the 1.0 contract): internal plain-C spec
  stage in the bridge; the build-time math and printing LANDED (slice
  S1, dec-A260): the engine and the support library call seven ext_ math
  names, R's own routines in the R package and a renamed copy of R
  4.6.2's nmath (standalone/nmath) under DBARTS_HOST_STANDALONE, and
  printing is chosen when the code is compiled, misc's print pointers
  gone; still owed, from slice S2a: the spec carrying the resolved
  residual-prior scale; a plain-spec creation entry and a host-neutral
  error contract in dbarts.h covering EVERY entry point, not creation
  alone (VD 2026-09-27, dec-B119): a refusal returns a status the host
  reads instead of raising an R error, the unwind to the entry point
  staying as it is; and a plain-C getter for each chain's current sigma,
  riding the same header change so consumers rebuild once (VD
  2026-09-27, dec-A73). The sister re-verification at the freeze
  follows it. Rewords the Python-binding entry.
  Predictor source consolidation (decided, VD 2026-09-24; dec-B124),
  same arc: the spec's predictor part is dbarts_predictor_source, so the
  R parse and the flat entries fill one struct and pass one
  translate-and-validate stage (translateSource, today flat-only) into
  bartcore::PredictorSource. The header takes the engine's addressing:
  dbarts_predictor_source gains denseChannels (per column, >= 0 double
  column, < 0 code column ~k), denseCodes is read only through it, and
  the kind-selected single columnSources index goes. Retires the
  per-bridge bounds and channel sweeps and the int32 -> ColumnKind /
  xint_t copies; columnTypes and categoryCounts gain a reader. Refusals
  that name a column by its R name stay in the R bridge. Owed: the
  in-tree C consumer test, an ABI hash re-bake, a stan4bart rebuild
  (dense helper only, no source change).

stan4bart-zero-one-weights (found landing frequency-weights)
  dbarts keeps a probit fit's 0/1 weights on its data as membership
  (dec-B569), so stan4bart's active.bart and setActiveRows code for 0/1
  weights can be dropped, about 30 lines in stan4bart; its test reads
  membership from the sampler's data weights.

engine constants audit (decided, VD 2026-09-08; docs/decisions.md
  dec-B91; LANDED; plan docs/plans/engine-performance.md)
  post-release: a size gate on the fused roll + suffstat pass, two
  thresholds rather than one - fuse weighted at n >= 1e4, unweighted at
  n >= 2e4 - since the fusion loses up to 8 percent below those and
  wins up to 24 above, and one threshold cannot serve both families
  (at n = 1e4 it would have to fuse weighted and decline unweighted);
  leaf occupancy is a worse key than n and is not even monotone in the
  gain. It must arrive WITH equivalence scenarios sized above the
  threshold: every fit in all three corpora today is under 2000 rows,
  so a gate would revert all 75 to the stock association and leave the
  fused pass with no equivalence coverage at all. Crossover grid and
  corpus count: docs/plans/engine-performance.md, "Crossover grid for
  a size gate".

engine generics deep dive (decided, VD 2026-09-08; docs/decisions.md
  dec-B92)
  an independent design memo on what the engine's generic axes should
  be (leaf kind, response family, forest count and coupling, response
  shape, threading), derived from the model space the package intends
  to cover rather than from the current headers, with the current
  shape critiqued against it. Written: docs/design/engine-generics-
  review.md. VD (2026-09-08): not reading it now; turn it into research
  with measurements down the road. Unscheduled.

rule draw default weight (decided, VD 2026-09-08; docs/decisions.md
  dec-B96)
  stays at weight zero through the merge; the 0.16 was agent-suggested
  without rationale. The post-merge mixing research sets the weight with
  a rationale, pre- or post-1.0-0 as time allows. Manual documents the
  kernel and its cost.

binary-hyperprior-revisit (raised by the study, 2026-09-14;
  docs/plans/binary-hyperprior.md, "What to revisit after the mixing
  work"; before 1.0-0, once k's scale move lands, dec-B390; whether it
  lands is k-move-pricing's, dec-B530)
  the sampled k does not converge at any length that study could pay
  for: 64,000 draws per fit reach an effective sample size of 29 and a
  split-Rhat that never falls below 1.2. Three of its results rest on
  that - every sampled-k number in the note is a chain-length summary
  rather than a posterior one, the residual 0.008 of coverage
  shortfall still showing at the probe could be the sampler or the
  model, and the interior optimum is located by coverage-distance
  differences of 0.002 to 0.005, the same order as the 0.012 the
  incumbent's own coverage still moved between the long length and the
  probe, so the optimum's location is not resolved to better than
  about a factor of two in scale. To reopen the default, a mixing fix
  has to show two things: the sampled k at a split-Rhat below 1.05
  with an effective sample size in the hundreds at a length a user
  would actually run, and then, at that length, either a spread in
  coverage distance across the finite-scale arms larger than 0.02 or
  an optimum somewhere other than scale 0.5 to 2. If the spread stays
  at half a point of coverage, no mixing fix reopens the question; it
  only makes the verdict better founded. Re-run with
  benchmarks/R/binary-hyperprior.R. The fixed-k results, the largest
  effects in the note, involve no sampled k and do not depend on this.

